    {
      "question": "How do I sell a superyacht?",
      "answer": "Most sellers appoint a central agent: one broker, under a fixed-term exclusive agreement, who markets the yacht while other brokers introduce buyers. The seller’s own lawyer should review that agreement, in particular the minimum marketing activity, the commission payable after it ends, and the governing law (Clyde & Co, The Guide to Superyacht Law). Before listing, assemble the maintenance records, the class status and the evidence of the yacht’s VAT position, and set the asking price against published days on market for the size band. The sale is usually made on the MYBA Memorandum of Agreement, with a deposit of typically 10 percent on signing."
    },
    {
      "question": "What does selling a yacht cost?",
      "answer": "The main cost is the broker’s commission, paid by the seller from the proceeds. Two norms are in use: the US flat 10 percent of the sale price, and the MYBA sliding scale of 10 percent on the first 10 million, 5 percent on the second 10 million and 2.5 percent above. On a EUR 28 million sale that is EUR 2.8 million, or about EUR 1.7 million. A buyer’s broker is paid out of the seller’s commission, typically half of it or a little less. Agree the scale, any split, and any commission payable after the agreement ends in writing before listing. The seller’s own legal fees come on top."
    },
    {
      "question": "What warranties does a seller give when selling a yacht?",
      "answer": "Under the MYBA Memorandum of Agreement the seller’s warranties are narrow: the right to sell, and delivery free of mortgages, liens and other claims, with exclusion clauses ruling out warranties on condition beyond what the agreement states (Andrew Charlier of HFW, in BOAT International, September 2025). The 2021 MYBA E-MoA also has the seller warrant that no undischarged tax or VAT liability has been incurred. The warranties are usually backed by a guarantee from the beneficial owner or a company on its behalf. Its scope, cap and expiry are negotiated, so the seller’s lawyer should agree them before signing."
